<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- ForeCastTab — the body of the ForeCast window: the SPC outlook (card + day / product / opacity) with
     its legend, then SPC's forecast discussion. One of three mode bodies (see Controls/Windows/TemporalWindow, registered once per mode);
     the window supplies the surface, the title and the pin, so this is content only.
     ⚠️ The file keeps its "Tab" name from when the three were tabs of one Timeframe window. It is the same
     control, unchanged by the split; renaming all three would be churn. Bound to the coordinator
     MapViewModel; every control here drives ViewModel.Outlook.

       ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │ ⌄  SPC outlook       ────────●──────   55%           │  ← the window's one section; its
       │                                                      │     OPACITY rides in the header
       │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
       │  │ Day 1 · Categorical                      │        │  the card: which outlook is
       │  │ Issued 12:57 PM · Valid → Wed 7:00 AM    │        │  drawn, when SPC issued it,
       │  │ Next ~9 min                              │        │  and the refresh countdown
       │  └──────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
       │  Day      [Day 1 · Wed Sep 2          ▾]             │  ← self-labeling; cascades Product
       │  Product  [Categorical                ▾]             │
       └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
         ──────────────────────────────────────────────────     ← the LEGEND: its own star row, so it
          ■ 2%                                             ▲     fills every pixel down to the discussion
          ■ 5%                                             █     and scrolls inside that; hides with
          ■ 10%                                            █     HasLegend OR the outlook section collapsed
          Intensity hatching                     [✓] Show  █  ← HasHatchLegend (none on Categorical/fire)
          ╲ CIG1                               In Outlook  █  ← status from the CACHED GeoJSON, so a
          ╱ CIG2                               In Outlook  █     hidden hatch can't be forgotten
          ╳ CIG3                           Not In Outlook  ▼  ← dimmed
       ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │ ›  Forecast discussion                               │  ← pinned to the BOTTOM; COLLAPSED on
       └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     load, opens into a 240px cap

       (expanded)
       ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │ ⌄  Forecast discussion                             ▲ │
       │  SPC AC 021257                                     █ │  SPC's own teletype prose,
       │  DAY 1 CONVECTIVE OUTLOOK                          █ │  fixed-pitch so its columns
       │  ...VALID 021300Z - 031200Z...                     ▼ │  and rules line up
       └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     TWO SECTIONS, two Primitives/PanelSections (the one header every section in the app wears, Settings
     and the other two temporal windows included). SPC outlook is open on load — it is
     what the window is for. Inside it: a summary CARD, then [label | control] rows in one aligned 60px
     column, then the legend. Forecast discussion is COLLAPSED on load; see its own note below. This was the loosest of the three bodies — no section structure
     at all, a local OutlookLabelStyle that was character-for-character the shared SettingsFieldLabelStyle,
     and a hand-rolled letter-spaced "Legend" label. All three are gone.
     ⚠️ The window sizes to its content ONCE, at open, so collapsing this section leaves the window at its
     full height and re-expanding it is fine, but a section that GROWS after open runs past the bottom. A
     hand-rolled scroller is coming; WinUI's is not wanted.

     ⚠️ NEITHER HEADER CARRIES A SELECT-ALL BOX, unlike NowCast's and PastCast's, and that is not an
     omission to be tidied up. A box up there is a LAYER's show/hide; the only boolean this window has is
     OutlookViewModel.IsOutlookVisible, which IS the ForeCast mode (MapViewModel.IsForeCast projects onto
     it), and a tab body never arms or clears its own mode — see the last ⚠️ in this header. The
     discussion is prose, not a layer at all. Nor a grip: one layer has nothing to re-order against.

     ⚠️ THE CARD COSTS NO VIEW-MODEL WORK. Its three lines are OutlookViewModel state that was already
     being computed and shown nowhere — the title, the issued/valid line, and the refresh countdown. See
     the card region in that class.

     ⚠️ THE LEGEND IS ITS OWN SECTION, not a block inside the outlook one: it is a key to what is on the
     map rather than a control, so giving it a heading is what stops it reading as a fourth field.

     ⚠️ THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO RADAR SECTION HERE, unlike Past and Now. Radar opacity is a GLOBAL value,
     and those two windows can only get away with sharing a control for it because their modes are
     mutually exclusive — they can never both be open. ForeCast COEXISTS with NowCast, so a slider here
     would be a second control writing the same value with both windows on screen.

     ⚠️ Opacity is DISABLED while Product is None — None is this section's off switch, the same rule the
     PastCast outlook and the storm-report dots follow.

     ⚠️ THE FORECAST DISCUSSION IS ITS OWN SECTION, collapsed, bounded. It was deferred for exactly as long
     as these bodies had no section structure to hang it on ("wants its own bounded section — likely to
     arrive with per-section Expanders"); the Expanders arrived, so it did. Collapsed because the window
     sizes to content ONCE at open and this prose runs to a length SPC picks. Don't open it by default and
     don't unbound it.

     ⚠️ This tab does NOT turn ForeCast on or off; that is the ForeCast key in the bottom bar's centre
     cluster. A tab body configures a mode, the key runs it. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Composites.ForeCastTab"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:conv="using:Anvil.Converters"
             xmlns:local="using:Anvil.Controls.Composites"
             xmlns:models="using:Anvil.Models"
             xmlns:prim="using:Anvil.Controls.Primitives"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <conv:HexToBrushConverter x:Key="HexToBrush"/>
        <conv:BoolToVisibilityConverter x:Key="BoolToVis"/>

        <!-- One legend row: the official NOAA colour swatch (fill + stroke from SpcRiskCatalog, harvested
             from SPC's published symbology) next to the level's own name.
             ⚠️ A Conditional Intensity Group is drawn as HATCHING over the fill, and the DIRECTION is the
             only thing telling the groups apart — every CIG level is black. So the swatch traces
             outlook.js's sig-hatch tiles exactly: two Paths, '' and '/', shown by the same back/fwd rule
             makeHatchImage uses, which makes CIG3 (both) fall out for free. Getting a direction wrong here
             makes the legend disagree with our own map AND with spc.noaa.gov.
             Item type: Anvil.Models.SpcRiskLevel. -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="OutlookLegendItemTemplate" x:DataType="models:SpcRiskLevel">
            <Grid Margin="0,2" ColumnSpacing="9">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <Grid Width="20" Height="13" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Border Background="{x:Bind Fill, Converter={StaticResource HexToBrush}}"
                            BorderBrush="{x:Bind Stroke, Converter={StaticResource HexToBrush}}"
                            BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="2"/>
                    <!-- '' — CIG1, and half of CIG3's cross. -->
                    <Path Stroke="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                          Visibility="{x:Bind local:ForeCastTab.HatchBackward(Hatch)}"
                          Data="M0,0 L13,13 M7,0 L20,13 M0,6.5 L6.5,13 M13,0 L20,6.5"/>
                    <!-- '/' — CIG2, and the other half of CIG3. -->
                    <Path Stroke="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                          Visibility="{x:Bind local:ForeCastTab.HatchForward(Hatch)}"
                          Data="M0,13 L13,0 M7,13 L20,0 M0,6.5 L6.5,0 M13,13 L20,6.5"/>
                </Grid>
                <!-- 13px, matching the type rows in the other two windows' sections. -->
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{x:Bind OfficialName}"
                           FontSize="13" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
            </Grid>
        </DataTemplate>

        <!-- One Conditional Intensity Group row: the hatch swatch (same two Paths + rule as above), its
             name, and whether today's outlook carries it. A group NOT in the outlook is dimmed as well as
             worded, so the "In Outlook" rows are the ones the eye lands on.
             Item type: Anvil.Models.OutlookHatchLegendRow. -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="OutlookHatchRowTemplate" x:DataType="models:OutlookHatchLegendRow">
            <Grid Margin="0,2" ColumnSpacing="9" Opacity="{x:Bind local:ForeCastTab.HatchRowOpacity(InOutlook)}">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <Grid Width="20" Height="13" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Border Background="{x:Bind Level.Fill, Converter={StaticResource HexToBrush}}"
                            BorderBrush="{x:Bind Level.Stroke, Converter={StaticResource HexToBrush}}"
                            BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="2"/>
                    <Path Stroke="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                          Visibility="{x:Bind local:ForeCastTab.HatchBackward(Level.Hatch)}"
                          Data="M0,0 L13,13 M7,0 L20,13 M0,6.5 L6.5,13 M13,0 L20,6.5"/>
                    <Path Stroke="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                          Visibility="{x:Bind local:ForeCastTab.HatchForward(Level.Hatch)}"
                          Data="M0,13 L13,0 M7,13 L20,0 M0,6.5 L6.5,0 M13,13 L20,6.5"/>
                </Grid>
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{x:Bind Level.OfficialName}"
                           FontSize="13" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{x:Bind StatusText}"
                           FontSize="12" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
            </Grid>
        </DataTemplate>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- ⚠️ NOT top-aligned, unlike the Past/Now roots: TemporalWindow hands this the full window height and
         the legend's star row is what fills it. -->
    <Grid RowSpacing="8">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- SPC outlook: the card, the selectors -->
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>    <!-- the legend: fills the rest, scrolls inside -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- Forecast discussion: SPC's prose, bounded, at the bottom -->
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- ===================== SPC OUTLOOK: the card, then the selectors ===================== -->
        <!-- No LayerId: this window has ONE layer, so there is nothing to re-order it against (the map slots
             it beside NowCast's stack — layers.js effectiveOrder). Opacity is in the header, greyed while
             Product is None: None is this section's off switch, the same rule as the PastCast outlook's. -->
        <prim:PanelSection x:Name="OutlookSection" Grid.Row="0" Header="SPC outlook" IsExpanded="True"
                           ShowOpacity="True"
                           OpacityValue="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.OutlookOpacity, Mode=TwoWay}"
                           IsOpacityEnabled="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.HasOutlook, Mode=OneWay}"
                           OpacityToolTip="Outlook fill opacity (outlines stay solid)">
            <Grid>
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- the card -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- rule -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- the selectors -->
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                <!-- THE CARD, the same shape as the PastCast outlook's and for the same reason: it is a
                     RESULT, not a preview — there is no Load here, the outlook applies the moment you pick it,
                     so it describes what is on the map right now. That is also why it carries no button. -->
                <Border Grid.Row="0" CornerRadius="8" Padding="13,12"
                        Background="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceHoverBrush}"
                        BorderThickness="1"
                        BorderBrush="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolidBrush}">
                    <StackPanel>
                        <TextBlock Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.CardHeadline, Mode=OneWay}"
                                   FontSize="15"
                                   Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.CardContext, Mode=OneWay}"
                                   FontSize="13" Margin="0,4,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                   Visibility="{x:Bind HasText(ViewModel.Outlook.CardContext), Mode=OneWay}"
                                   Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
                        <TextBlock Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.CardFooter, Mode=OneWay}"
                                   FontSize="11" Margin="0,6,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                   Visibility="{x:Bind HasText(ViewModel.Outlook.CardFooter), Mode=OneWay}"
                                   Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorTertiaryBrush}"/>
                    </StackPanel>
                </Border>

                <Border Grid.Row="1" Height="1" Margin="0,14"
                        Background="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolidBrush}"/>

                <Grid Grid.Row="2" ColumnSpacing="12" RowSpacing="10">
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="60"/>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- Day -->
                        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- Product -->
                    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                    <!-- Day: self-labeling ("Day 1 · Sat Jun 14"); changing it cascades the product list.
                         ⚠️ A ComboBox, not a SegmentedPicker like PastCast's Day row — the live outlook runs to
                         EIGHT days (d4-8 are the extended probabilistic), which is well past what that
                         primitive suits. -->
                    <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="Day" Style="{StaticResource SettingsFieldLabelStyle}"/>
                    <ComboBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                              ItemsSource="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.Days}"
                              DisplayMemberPath="Label"
                              SelectedItem="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.SelectedDayOption, Mode=TwoWay}"
                              ToolTipService.ToolTip="SPC outlook day"/>

                    <!-- Product: the day's outlook types (+ None). The list CASCADES with the day, so it stays
                         a dropdown — its length changes. -->
                    <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Text="Product" Style="{StaticResource SettingsFieldLabelStyle}"/>
                    <ComboBox Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                              ItemsSource="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.ProductOptions, Mode=OneWay}"
                              DisplayMemberPath="Label"
                              SelectedItem="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.SelectedOption, Mode=TwoWay}"
                              ToolTipService.ToolTip="Outlook product (None clears the overlay)"/>
                </Grid>

            </Grid>
        </prim:PanelSection>

        <!-- ===================== THE LEGEND: fills the window ===================== -->
        <!-- A key to the outlook the section above chose, so it hides when that section is collapsed as well
             as on HasLegend (None, or the layer off). It sits OUTSIDE the section only so it can take a star
             row — a section's body sizes to itself, and the old 200px cap is what cut the list off.
             ⚠️ Its ScrollViewer is bounded by the ROW, not a MaxHeight: the window is full height, so the
             legend gets every pixel between the selectors and the discussion and only scrolls past that.
             ⚠️ The hatching block reads "In Outlook" from the CACHED file, not the catalog — that is the whole
             point: with hatching hidden, those rows are the reminder that it exists. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="1"
              Visibility="{x:Bind LegendVisibility(ViewModel.Outlook.HasLegend, OutlookSection.IsExpanded), Mode=OneWay}">
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- rule -->
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>    <!-- entries -->
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <Border Grid.Row="0" Height="1" Margin="0,6,0,10"
                    Background="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolidBrush}"/>
            <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" HorizontalScrollMode="Disabled">
                <StackPanel>
                    <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.LegendEntries, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  ItemTemplate="{StaticResource OutlookLegendItemTemplate}"/>

                    <!-- Hatching heading + the Show box. Absent on products with no intensity groups. -->
                    <Grid Margin="0,12,0,2" ColumnSpacing="8"
                          Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.HasHatchLegend, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}, Mode=OneWay}">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <TextBlock Text="Intensity hatching" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   Style="{StaticResource SettingsFieldLabelStyle}"/>
                        <CheckBox Grid.Column="1" Content="Show" MinWidth="0"
                                  IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.ShowHatching, Mode=TwoWay}"
                                  ToolTipService.ToolTip="Show or hide the hatched intensity areas (fills stay)"/>
                    </Grid>
                    <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.HatchLegendRows, Mode=OneWay}"
                                  ItemTemplate="{StaticResource OutlookHatchRowTemplate}"/>
                </StackPanel>
            </ScrollViewer>
        </Grid>

        <!-- ===================== FORECAST DISCUSSION: SPC's own prose ===================== -->
        <!-- SPC's forecast discussion for the selected day, scraped from their HTML product page and disk
             cached (OutlookViewModel.OutlookNarrativeText ← SpcOutlookService.GetNarrativeAsync). It was
             fetched on every selection change and every 15-min refresh cycle for years while being shown
             NOWHERE; this section is where it finally lands.
             ⚠️ COLLAPSED ON LOAD, unlike the outlook section — and that is the whole design. The window sizes
             to its content ONCE, at open, so a section that is open at that moment sets the window height.
             This is long prose of a length SPC decides, so opening by default would size the window to a
             stranger's paragraph count and hit TemporalWindow's work-area clamp.
             ⚠️ BOUNDED for the same reason: MaxHeight + an inner scroller, the same treatment the legend
             above gets. These two are the only scrollers in the temporal windows, and they are NOT the
             window-body ScrollViewer that TemporalWindow deliberately refuses — they are inner regions whose
             CONTENT LENGTH IS NOT OURS TO DECIDE. Don't generalise either one into a body scroller.
             ⚠️ DISABLED while Product is None, the same off switch the opacity slider uses. Fire-weather
             products have no scraped page (their layout differs); the text says so itself rather than the
             section hiding, so the row never silently vanishes. -->
        <prim:PanelSection Grid.Row="2" Header="Forecast discussion" IsExpanded="False"
                           IsEnabled="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.HasOutlook, Mode=OneWay}">
            <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" MaxHeight="240"
                          HorizontalScrollMode="Disabled">
                <!-- Monospace: SPC ships the discussion as pre-formatted teletype text (ExtractPreText pulls
                     it out of a <pre> block), so its columns and hand-drawn separators only line up in a
                     fixed-pitch face. IsTextSelectionEnabled so a forecaster line can be copied out. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.Outlook.OutlookNarrativeText, Mode=OneWay}"
                           FontFamily="Consolas" FontSize="12" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           IsTextSelectionEnabled="True"
                           Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>
            </ScrollViewer>
        </prim:PanelSection>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
